    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Opponents of reparations for descendants of enslaved people often argue that giving reparations would unfairly penalize current generations who had no direct role in slavery or the US institution of slavery. Others claim that reparations may not effectively address historical injustices, as the legacy of slavery can be felt across generations through societal and economic disparities. Some also argue that reparations could be seen as meaningless or insufficient if funds are not matched by corresponding actions to address ongoing inequality. They might also point to the unavailability of a comprehensive database to accurately identify living descendants of enslaved people.",
