      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Opponents of reparations argue that current generations are unfairly penalized because the affected enslaved individuals and their families no longer exist. Under the US Constitution, specifically the 13th Amendment to the Constitution, states that individuals involved in the slave trade or slavery themselves cannot be sued or compensated. Additionally, Supreme Court cases such as Fanning v. Garney (1858) and Loving v. Virginia (1967) established that Caesarean babies of enslaved mothers were considered to be born into bondage and therefore not for free. These legal precedents argue that any responsibility for chattel slavery that is passed down should be to the individuals or entities at the time of that enslavement, rather than descendants of those individuals.",
